# AoE fig 3.27 — cascode JFET current sink

**Source:** Horowitz &amp;amp; Hill, *The Art of Electronics*, 3rd ed., fig 3.27, p. 146
(PDF 179; §3.2.2 discussion just before §3.2.3).

## What it shows

A **two-terminal** current sink: Q1 is the ordinary self-biased sink (its gate on
the bottom rail, its source lifted by R_S, so V_GS1 = −I·R_S
sets the current on Q1's transfer curve). Q2 sits on top of it **as a cascode**:
its gate is tied to Q1's **source** (no bias network needed — depletion mode),
so Q2's gate–source voltage equals minus Q1's drain–source voltage and
**V_DS1 = −V_GS2**: Q1 runs at a fixed, healthy V_DS no matter what
the load does. Q2 absorbs the load-voltage swing, so the current sees none of it
through Q1's λ (channel-length modulation): the book's point is that this "nice
additional benefit" makes the pair a two-terminal source that is much **stiffer**
than the simple self-biased sink of fig 3.23, at the cost of ~1 V extra
compliance voltage (V_DS1 plus Q2's saturation margin).

## Parts

| Part | Value | Source |
|---|---|---|
| Q2 (JFET, cascode, on top) | Model 2N5485 (SimulIDE: I_DSS 14 mA, V_p −2 V, λ 0.01/V) | figure prints **J111**; [chosen] 2N5485 — the next-stiffer Siliconix model, mirroring the book's bigger-FET-on-top intent. SimulIDE's J111 model drives the solver into a non-converged state (see limits) |
| Q1 (JFET, current-setting) | Model 2N5484 (I_DSS 6.75 mA, V_p −1.5 V, λ 0.01/V) | figure prints **J112**; [chosen] 2N5484 per this repo's AoE JFET convention (figs 3.20, 3.23 rows); same solver problem with J-series here |
| RS | 220 Ω | [INFERRED] — the figure names R_S only; 220 Ω puts the design point at ≈2.6 mA, between the fig 3.20 (I_DSS, 7.3 mA) and fig 3.23 (1 mA) rows |
| RL (load) | 1 kΩ | [chosen] — the figure draws only two terminal circles; load as in the fig 3.20/3.23 rows |
| +15 V rail | 15 V | [INFERRED] — rails [INFERRED], as in those rows |

*Datasheet-spread caveat:* real J111/J112 run far larger currents (I_DSS
tens of mA); with the printed parts and R_S the book gives no values, so the
operating current here is the model's, not a book number.

## How to use it

Run; the sink self-biases in the first time step (V_SNS rises to I·R_S).
Sweep the load with `cli comp set RL Resistance=…` and the rail with
`cli comp set V1 Voltage=…`. **Sweep by continuation:** let it settle at a
known-good load first, then change RL while the sim is paused and run on — a
cold start at some RL values (2.2 k, 3.3 k, 4.3 k here) can land the solver in a
dead fixed point (RL current 0, Q2 OFF); re-starting fixes it, and the saved
design point (RL 1 k) cold-starts cleanly.

## Results [SIMULATED]

- **Design point (RL = 1 kΩ, +15 V): I_out = 2.6063 mA**, V(RS) = 0.5734 V =
  |V_GS1| — exactly the Shockley point for the 2N5484 model
  (V_GS = −1.5 V×(1−√(2.6063/6.75×1.0118)) = −0.573 V; R_S =
  0.5734 V/2.6063 mA = 220.0 Ω — the self-bias loop closes on the value chosen).
  **The cascode identity holds exactly: V_GS2 = −V_DS1 = 1.180 V**
  (Shockley alone gives 1.14 V; Q2's λ at V_DS2 = 10.6 V lifts it — the
  measured 1.180 V matches 2×(1−√(2.6063/(14×1.1064))) to three digits).
  Both FETs ACTIVE. KCL exact: RL, RS and both channels carry 2.6063 mA.
- **Load sweep (RL 100 Ω → 4.7 kΩ, +15 V): I_out 2.6064 → 2.6058 mA, −0.023 %**
  (r_out ≥ 20 MΩ — flat to the last printed digit; the floor is the readout's
  resolution, the true r_out of r_ds2(1+g_m2r_ds1) ≈ 9 GΩ is
  far below what the readout can see). Compliance edge: RL = 4.7 kΩ still
  regulates (TOP node 2.75 V; Q2's V_DS down to 1.04 V, its saturation
  margin); **RL = 5.6 kΩ → 2.426 mA (−6.9 %, both FETs leave saturation), 10 kΩ →
  1.434 mA**. The sink complies with ≳2.4 V across it.
- **Rail sweep (RL = 1 kΩ, +6 → +30 V): I_out 2.6059 → 2.6068 mA, +0.03 %**
  (r_out ≈ 30 MΩ by the same resolution floor). 5 V rail still regulates (Q2
  barely OHMIC, I holds); **4 V → 2.489 mA (−4.5 %), 3 V → 1.995 mA**.
- **Stiffness, against this repo's rows:** fig 3.20 (I = I_DSS, no R_S, no
  cascode) drooped 7.71 → 6.92 mA = **−10 %**, r_out ≈ 15 kΩ; fig 3.23 (self-biased
  2N5484) drooped **−2.9 %**, r_out ≈ 430 kΩ. This cascode: **−0.023 %**, r_out
  ≥ 20 MΩ — ≈50× stiffer than the same-JFET self-biased sink, ≈10³× vs I_DSS.
  The price: compliance ~2.4 V vs ~1.5 V for both simple sinks (V_DS1 is
  held *up* at −V_GS2 ≈ 1.2 V rather than sitting near pinch-off).

## What to try [SIMULATED]

- Raise R_S (`comp set RS Resistance=470`) → I falls to ≈1.4 mA and
  V_DS1 = −V_GS2 **grows** (Q2 needs more back-bias at less current): the
  compliance voltage cost rises as you trim the current down.
- Swap Q2 to Model=2N5484 (same part as Q1): V_DS1 drops to ≈0.9 V — the
  sink gets *more* compliant and slightly *less* stiff; that trade is exactly
  why the book draws a beefier FET on top.
- Watch the cascode buy its stiffness: at RL = 4.7 kΩ, Q2's V_DS has eaten
  12 V of swing while Q1's V_DS moved 0.01 V (1.180 → 1.142 V).
- `comp set Q1 Model=J112`: SimulIDE's J-series model breaks the solver here —
  the point of the model substitution above.

## Warnings left

None (rounds 1–2: 0 warnings, 0 crossings). The model substitution is recorded
under Parts and in the saved limits.

## create-circuit skill

- Version: 1.20.1 (commit a75fa3b)
- Circuit: fig3_27_cascode_jfet_sink.sim1

### Limitations found while building it

- SimulIDE J-series JFET models (J111, J112) drive the solver non-convergent in this stacked-cascode circuit: [#362](https://github.com/jeroenwk/create-circuit/issues/362)
- Solver cold-start lands in a dead fixed point at some load values (fresh start, RL 2.2k/3.3k/4.3k observed): [#363](https://github.com/jeroenwk/create-circuit/issues/363)
</description>


